{
    "background_story": "Lucia grew up in a small village in Africa where geophagy, the practice of eating earth, was a common tradition among pregnant women. According to local custom, consuming clay from the nearby riverbank would alleviate morning sickness and ensure a healthy pregnancy. Lucia's grandmother had done it, and her mother had done it, so when Lucia became pregnant with her first child, she followed suit. She would often sneak away to the riverbank, digging up small handfuls of clay to mix with water and drink. The villagers believed that the earth possessed healing properties, and Lucia found that it indeed soothed her queasy stomach. As her belly grew, so did her connection to the land and her ancestors, who had practiced geophagy for generations. Lucia's experience with geophagy became a symbol of her cultural heritage and her deep bond with the natural world.",
